<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Roland Brownlee - Full Pack Part 3</title></head><body><h1>Roland Brownlee - Full Pack Part 3</h1><p>Roland Brownlee — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 3)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>04-08 Budgeting Strategy</p><p>Plaud file id: 3ca0ff7fc73554aaedb44ddb14348ac3</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Financial Year Budgeting and Workflow Strategy Meeting</p><p>This document outlines a comprehensive strategy meeting between Bob Duncan, Roland Brownlee, and Megan Gordon, focused on evaluating Roland&#x27;s current financial year performance and setting ambitious financial targets for the upcoming year. The discussion culminated in a mutual agreement on a £178,680 income target—underpinned by specific case conversion metrics—and introduced the new &quot;Platform&quot; workflow management system to ensure operational continuity and robust lead management, particularly in light of Megan&#x27;s upcoming maternity leave.</p><p>---</p><p>## Current Year Performance Review</p><p>The meeting commences with brief informal pleasantries, briefly touching upon Megan Gordon recovering from a slipped rib caused by hypermobility, before swiftly transitioning into a data-driven evaluation of Roland Brownlee&#x27;s performance for the current financial year. Bob Duncan presents a comprehensive spreadsheet that focuses explicitly on banked performance rather than physical written business to establish a clear baseline.</p><p>As it stands, Roland&#x27;s banked income totals approximately £32,000. This figure is comprised of £16,000 in mortgage income, £1,200 in renewals, £7,000 in insurance, and approximately £7,500 in additional fees. Bob notes that Roland did not previously have a formal budget framework, having recently joined the branch to establish his pipeline and get things moving. While the current projections assume marginal growth for the remainder of the year, Bob emphasises that these figures will be dynamically refreshed as the financial year concludes to reflect Roland&#x27;s true run rate.</p><p>---</p><p>## Collaborative Target Setting for the Next Financial Year</p><p>A core component of the meeting involves Bob and Roland collaboratively establishing a robust financial framework for the upcoming year, ultimately agreeing on a comprehensive income target of £178,680. To ensure this high bar feels achievable, the target is meticulously broken down into distinct, manageable metrics.</p><p>Looking ahead, Roland has 48 pending renewal cases. Assuming a 60% conversion rate (split evenly between full remortgages and product transfers), this is projected to generate £29,952. Bob factors in the likelihood of Roland cross-selling insurance on these renewals, as many currently lack coverage. Furthermore, Roland’s average admin fee sits at £397, with a 79% conversion rate (rounded to 80% for projections). While Roland feels confident he could increase this fee on new purchases, he acknowledges the difficulty with renewals—which often lacked initial charges—and agrees to maintain the baseline assumption.</p><p>Regarding new business, Bob notes Roland&#x27;s recent success in averaging two purchase cases per week. However, projecting this across 48 weeks (allowing for holidays) yields 96 cases, which Roland views as overly steep alongside his remortgage workload. Consequently, they negotiate a more realistic target of 1.5 purchase cases per week, equating to 72 cases for the year (or roughly six per month). At an average income of £737 per purchase case, this will generate £53,000 in mortgage income. Coupled with £57,000 projected from insurance (based on a strict conversion rate of one-in-two for purchases and one-in-four for renewals, averaging £1,199 per case) and £38,000 from admin fees, the calculations align perfectly with the £178,680 goal. Roland expresses that breaking the total down demystifies the target, firmly preferring the higher value of new purchases over relying on lower-yielding product transfers.</p><p>---</p><p>## Identifying Strategic Dependencies and Support Requirements</p><p>To guarantee the feasibility of the newly established targets, the discussion pivots to identifying the strategic dependencies that will dictate Roland&#x27;s success. Bob highlights that the primary variable will be the volume and consistency of high-quality leads. Currently, Roland receives an aggregate of between 249 and 480 leads, but to comfortably hit the target volume of £200,000 in written/banked projections, he needs to be actively speaking with 30 to 35 high-quality leads per month and achieving a one-in-three conversion rate. Both agree that maintaining this lead quality is paramount.</p><p>Roland explicitly outlines his core support requirements, requesting continued weekly check-ins and stressing that a steady influx of leads is his absolute priority. Moreover, he underscores his heavy reliance on administrative support, praising the strong working relationship he has cultivated with Megan. As his case volume increases, Roland admits it is impossible to manage the administrative burden independently. He flags Megan&#x27;s impending maternity leave as a significant operational risk, emphasising the absolute necessity of maintaining robust administrative cover to sustain his momentum, delegate pipeline tasks, and handle the projected case volume effectively.</p><p>---</p><p>## Introduction of the &quot;Platform&quot; Workflow Management System</p><p>Addressing Roland&#x27;s concerns regarding administrative continuity, Bob provides a comprehensive demonstration of &quot;Platform,&quot; a new workflow management system slated to go live on the 20th of the month, with formal training to follow shortly. Bob presents Platform as a streamlined, task-driven alternative to the convoluted architecture of the Midas system, specifically designed to automate client journeys and facilitate seamless collaboration between advisors and administrative staff.</p><p>The system operates on an automated workflow driven by explicit tasks. For instance, following an appointment, Roland is prompted to log the outcome. If a client does not answer, the task automatically routes to admin for follow-up and nurturing. For clients who are &quot;actively looking,&quot; the system orchestrates a cyclic communication loop alternating between Roland and the admin team. Clients unready to proceed are placed into a six-month nurture journey, receiving monthly automated emails alongside three scheduled admin calls; if they re-engage, they are automatically booked back into the advisor&#x27;s diary. Bob stresses that leads should only be marked &quot;Not Proceeding With&quot; (NPW) if the client explicitly requests it.</p><p>While Megan expresses a slight preference for the visual pie charts of their previous system, she acknowledges that Platform&#x27;s list-based interface will mandate consistent communication and keep advisors actively engaged in file updates. The system also extends into comprehensive mortgage workflows, managing post-completion touchpoints (admin calls at one day post-completion, and advisor calls at three days for reviews), down valuations, rekeys, and automated nine-month protection callbacks. Notably, Platform empowers clients to complete their own fact-finds online, signaling upfront commitment. Furthermore, it allows advisors to run full affordability checks across major lenders—such as Halifax, Nationwide, Barclays, and NatWest—and submit Decision in Principles (DIPs) and full applications without the need to rekey data externally. It also features a &quot;mortgage passport,&quot; providing clients with an easily downloadable DIP.</p><p>---</p><p>## Final Commitment and Implementation Plan</p><p>The meeting concludes with a solid consensus on the operational plan. Having reviewed the granular breakdown of the figures and the supportive infrastructure provided by Platform, Roland confirms his final commitment to the £178,680 financial target. He fully owns the objective of securing 1.5 new purchase cases per week alongside a 50% insurance conversion rate, agreeing that the goals are entirely manageable once the new workflow system is fully integrated.</p><p>To formalise this commitment, Bob outlines the immediate next steps: he will calculate the specific quarterly targets for both written and banked income, as well as the required number of monthly cases. These metrics will serve as the core benchmark for their ongoing weekly check-ins, providing Roland with clear, incremental targets to ensure he maintains his highly successful run rate throughout the new financial year.</p><p>---</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Bob Duncan**</p><p>- [ ] Calculate the specific quarterly targets for written income, banked income, and case numbers, and share the comprehensive breakdown with Roland Brownlee - [End of next week]</p><p>**@Roland Brownlee**</p><p>- [ ] Complete practice navigation and workflow testing within the new &quot;Platform&quot; system using the designated test case &quot;John Smith&quot; - [TBD]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Roland Brownlee (Manager: Bob Duncan)</p><p>*Content creation date: 2026-04-08 10:00:32*</p><p>*Generated by gpt-5.2*</p><p>---</p><p>## Sales Performance (Targets &amp; Run Rate)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Agreed annual income target: **£178,680**, comprised of:</p><p>  - **72 purchase cases** (**1.5 new cases/week** = **6 purchase cases/month**)</p><p>  - **48 renewals**</p><p>  - **48 insurance cases**</p><p>  - **96 admin fees**</p><p>- Current written-business run rate referenced:</p><p>  - Last 3 months written: **£42k** → annualised approx **£168k** (below **£178,680**)</p><p>- Written vs banked dependency referenced:</p><p>  - To bank **£178k**, written requirement discussed: **~£195k total written**, averaging **just over £16k/month**</p><p>- Banked performance (current year-to-date) stated:</p><p>  - Mortgage income: **£16,000**</p><p>  - Renewal income: **£1,200**</p><p>  - Insurance: **£7,000**</p><p>  - Additional income line noted: **~£7,500**</p><p>  - **Total banked (stated): ~£32,000**</p><p>- Purchases banked so far this year: **22**</p><p>- Conversion assumptions referenced:</p><p>  - Purchases protection: **1 in 2**</p><p>  - Renewals protection reviews: **1 in 4**</p><p>  - Admin fee conversion: **80%** (current noted as **79%**); average admin fee stated: **£397** (including zero-fee cases)</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available** (no prior weekly/monthly comparator figures provided in the content).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Conversion described as strong: **“converting well on opportunities”**.</p><p>- Purchase volume capability evidenced by monthly sequence referenced: **3, 2, 4, 1 (December), 4, 3, 6** (with **6** identified as required monthly pace).</p><p>- Target calibration improved realism: moved from **2 cases/week** to an “owned” planning target of **1.5/week** to reflect renewal workload.</p><p>- Income modelling anchored to current run-rate assumptions (fee conversion and average admin fee figures stated above), rather than optimistic step-changes.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Consistency risk: plan requires **6 purchases every month**, not occasional spikes.</p><p>- Reporting inconsistency in application mix:</p><p>  - Stated **“74 apps this financial year”** but also **“24 purchases”** and **“15 remortgages”** noted as not reconciling (likely **NTUs** included).</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If purchase volume drops below **6/month**, risk to delivering **72 purchases/year** and therefore **£178,680**.</p><p>- Written-to-banked slippage exposure: needing **~£195k written** to bank **£178k** increases risk if progression slows.</p><p>- Reporting risk: if apps vs NTUs are miscategorised, capacity and income planning may be built on an incorrect baseline.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>1. Build a **quarter-by-quarter written vs banked forecast** (using prior banking pattern referenced).</p><p>2. Build a **purchase seasonality forecast** to manage quieter periods referenced (e.g., **November–January**).</p><p>3. Investigate and fix the **apps/purchases/remortgages reporting mismatch** (likely NTUs included).</p><p>4. Confirm and “own” the 2026 new business target: **72 purchase cases/year** (≈ **1.5/week**).</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Forecasting &amp; seasonality: **Other (Bob Duncan)**</p><p>- Target ownership (weekly pace): **Adviser (Roland Brownlee)**</p><p>- Reporting fix (apps vs split incl. NTUs): **Other (Bob / MI)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (conditional)** — run rate described as strong, and target ownership is more realistic (**1.5/week**), but delivery remains dependent on consistent monthly purchases and resolving reporting integrity issues.</p><p>---</p><p>## Leads Performance Review (Lead Supply &amp; CRM Discipline)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Lead volume requirement/constraint referenced:</p><p>  - Desired: **40**</p><p>  - Current: **25**</p><p>  - Typical average: **15–20** (give or take)</p><p>- Lead model referenced to support output:</p><p>  - **~480 leads/year**</p><p>  - **30–35 spoken-to leads/month**</p><p>  - **~1 in 3 conversion** (lead-to-application)</p><p>  - Implied output stated verbally: **~10 applications/month** from engaged leads</p><p>- Execution/process upgrade discussed:</p><p>  - Platform task-driven workflow for follow-up and nurture hand-offs between **Roland** and **Megan (admin)**.</p><p>  - Rules referenced: “no answer” → pushed to admin; “early looking” → moved to nurture; “actively looking” → alternating follow-ups (Roland ↔ Megan ↔ Roland).</p><p>  - Stated: **only 3 call attempts over a 6-month window** before lead is NPWed (unless re-engaged).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available** (no weekly/monthly lead outcomes, contact speed metrics, or CRM audit metrics provided).</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Clear identification of the main input constraint: **lead consistency**.</p><p>- Upstream pipeline indicators acknowledged (intent to monitor): **Mortgage appointments requested (MAs), referrals, home reports, viewings**.</p><p>- Workflow introduces structured ownership and reduces reliance on memory:</p><p>  - Adviser focuses on **due tasks**</p><p>  - Outcomes and notes are expected at each stage</p><p>- Clear linkage established: lead volume → conversations → conversion → applications → income.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Lead flow explicitly below level required for target confidence (**15–20 average vs 40 desired**).</p><p>- No quantitative evidence provided of CRM hygiene (e.g., note quality, stale statuses, 7+ day gaps, first-contact speed), creating a measurement blind spot.</p><p>- Ownership gap risk explicitly stated: if the adviser submits a case/updates but **does not progress the Platform task**, the case does not transition into admin ownership.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Revenue risk if lead volume/quality doesn’t increase or conversion/output doesn’t improve from current lead levels.</p><p>- Reliance on others for lead generation noted: **Alan** referenced as driving lead supply.</p><p>- Admin continuity risk: concerns raised regarding **Megan’s maternity leave**; if admin coverage changes, follow-up and nurture may degrade.</p><p>- Adoption risk: Platform is list-based (no “pie chart” view), which may reduce daily engagement and therefore follow-up consistency.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>1. Validate whether lead flow can support **~480/year** and whether adviser capacity supports **30–35 conversations/month** alongside renewals.</p><p>2. Track upstream activity and convert it into booked adviser appointments consistently (MAs/referrals/home reports/viewings → booked appointments).</p><p>3. Implement the **Platform task-based workflow** as standard practice for follow-ups and nurture hand-offs.</p><p>4. Prepare for Platform timeline events explicitly referenced:</p><p>   - Platform update goes live on **2026-04-20**</p><p>   - Training referenced as **2026-05-06**</p><p>5. Confirm admin cover plan given Megan maternity leave risk, aligned to workflow adoption.</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Lead supply/drive: **Other (Alan)**</p><p>- Lead flow modelling validation: **Other (Bob) + Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>- Day-to-day follow-up tasks + outcomes/note updates: **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>- Nurture journey management/rebooking once tasks bounce: **Admin (Megan / Admin team)**</p><p>- Workflow rollout/enablement + admin cover plan: **Other (Bob Duncan / Ops/Management)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (input constrained)** — lead volume is below the desired level (**25 current; 15–20 typical vs 40 desired**), despite strong stated conversion.</p><p>- **Improving (process upgrade)** — task-based workflow is being introduced, but effectiveness is adoption-dependent and admin-resourcing-dependent.</p><p>---</p><p>## “Where Are My Leads?” (Relevant)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Lead shortfall is positioned as the main constraint:</p><p>  - To hit **72 cases/year**, lead flow must either **double at the same quality**, or improve conversion/output (“keep it the same and write more”).</p><p>- Reporting dependency raised: lead-to-app modelling requires reliable reporting; application mix inconsistency exists (apps vs purchases/remortgages split).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available**.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Clear recognition that lead volume/quality is the upstream driver of purchase consistency and income delivery.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Lead stats are discussed without supporting CRM outcome reporting (no stated DIP/application/lost/uncontacted breakdowns).</p><p>- Application reporting inconsistency undermines confidence in lead-to-output measurement.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If lead supply or engagement drops below assumptions, sustaining **1.5 purchases/week** becomes high risk.</p><p>- Reporting inconsistency (apps vs split, NTUs) creates a planning and accountability risk.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Resolve reporting inconsistency (apps vs purchases/remortgages; NTUs).</p><p>- Validate feasible lead flow levels (current vs desired) and operational plan to close the gap.</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Reporting fix: **Other (Bob)**</p><p>- Lead flow validation: **Other (Bob) + Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — dependency is acknowledged, but input/output control metrics and reporting integrity are not yet resolved within the provided content.</p><p>---</p><p>## CRM Discipline (Notes, Updates, Ownership) — Platform Workflow Adoption</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Platform task-driven workflow discussed to improve:</p><p>  - Client touchpoints (adviser/admin “bounce” tasks)</p><p>  - Visibility/ownership of next steps</p><p>  - Progression from lead → DIP → application → completion → reviews</p><p>- Current preference noted for Midas due to visual “pie chart”; Platform viewed as “all lists”.</p><p>- Example clients referenced:</p><p>  - **John Smith** (demo file): used to illustrate workflow prompts and stages (including down-valuation decision prompts).</p><p>  - **Patrick Edington**: client completed fact find themselves; affordability check run in Platform and max loan decision result obtained.</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available**.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Workflow has defined post-completion cadence:</p><p>  - Admin call **1 day after completion**</p><p>  - Adviser call **3 days after completion** (thanks + review/referral)</p><p>  - Annual review workflow plus **3-month completion call**</p><p>  - If no policy is on risk, client routes to **protection 9-month callback list**</p><p>- Platform supports end-to-end activity within a client record (fact find, EIDV, documents, affordability, DIP, application steps).</p><p>- Client self-completion evidence (Patrick Edington) indicates improved engagement and reduced admin burden.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- UI friction risk: Platform lacks the “pie chart” view that supports daily tracking.</p><p>- Explicit ownership gap risk:</p><p>  - If adviser submits/acts but **does not move the task forward**, admin does not receive ownership and processing can stall.</p><p>- Admin visibility concern raised by Megan: advisers may not keep admin fully up to date (stated as a general issue in this review).</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Stalled hand-offs if “click done” discipline is inconsistent.</p><p>- Processing delays and unclear accountability if tasks are not progressed and notes not added after call attempts.</p><p>- Admin coverage risk due to Megan maternity leave concern.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>1. Use Platform task list **daily** (explicitly positioned as the replacement control point for checking the Midas pie chart at **17:00**).</p><p>2. Complete tasks and add a note after call attempts so the workflow bounces correctly between adviser/admin.</p><p>3. Roland and Megan to practice in the demo file **John Smith** (add/remove journeys; navigation familiarity).</p><p>4. Encourage clients to complete the fact find online (as per **Patrick Edington**) to increase commitment and speed to DIP.</p><p>5. Prepare for Platform update/training timeline:</p><p>   - Go-live: **2026-04-20**</p><p>   - Training: **2026-05-06**</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Daily task completion + notes: **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>- Nurture/rebooking once tasks bounce: **Admin (Megan / Admin team)**</p><p>- Practice session in John Smith demo: **Adviser (Roland) + Admin (Megan)**</p><p>- Workflow rollout/enablement: **Other (Bob / Ops)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (process clarity)** — workflow introduces structured touchpoints and ownership hand-offs.</p><p>- **At risk of stagnation (adoption-dependent)** — effectiveness depends on consistent task progression and note discipline; admin continuity is a stated risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## Renewal / Remortgage Income (Forward View)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Renewals pending next year: **48 cases**</p><p>- Conversion assumption: **~60%**</p><p>- Mix: **~50/50** full remortgage vs product transfer (PT)</p><p>- Modelled renewal income at 60%: **£29,952**</p><p>- Renewals fee constraints noted: charging consistency is “tricky” where clients weren’t charged previously.</p><p>- Opportunity identified: revisit protection on renewal book (“fresh set of eyes”).</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available**.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Quantified renewal base (48) with a defined conversion benchmark (60%).</p><p>- Identified opportunity to re-broke protection as part of renewal activity.</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Fee uplift is constrained by historic charging decisions on renewals.</p><p>- PT-heavy outcomes may reduce income per case vs full remortgage (implied in discussion).</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If conversion falls below **60%** and/or renewals skew towards PT, renewal income may undershoot the model.</p><p>- Limited ability to increase fees on renewals where not historically charged.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Prioritise review approach on renewals: identify which clients are suitable for **full remortgage vs PT**, and where **protection re-broking** is realistic.</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Renewal review planning and execution: **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant** — opportunity is recognised, but no client-level plan, booked reviews, or contact progress metrics are provided in the content.</p><p>---</p><p>## Projected Income (Budgeting / 2026 Planning)</p><p>### Current Position</p><p>- Banked performance (current year-to-date) stated: **~£32,000** total (see breakdown under Sales Performance).</p><p>- 2026 planning model discussed:</p><p>  - Purchases: **72/year** (≈ **1.5/week**)</p><p>  - Renewals: **48 cases**, **60%** conversion → **£29,952**</p><p>  - Lead requirement model referenced: **~480/year**, **30–35 conversations/month**, **~1 in 3 conversion**</p><p>- Fee strategy discussion:</p><p>  - Renewals kept conservative (fees not always previously charged)</p><p>  - Potential to “squeeze more” from fees on **purchases** where appropriate</p><p>### Vs Last Week / Last Month (if available)</p><p>- **Not available**.</p><p>### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Projection is linked to explicit activity drivers (cases/week, lead conversations, conversion assumptions).</p><p>- Fee assumptions are grounded in stated current conversion (admin fee conversion **~79–80%**) and average admin fee (**£397**).</p><p>### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Data integrity issue (apps vs purchases/remortgages split; likely NTUs) undermines baseline accuracy for projections.</p><p>### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Lead volume dependency is a single point of failure (model assumes **~480 leads/year** and sustained engagement).</p><p>- Reporting inconsistency may cause mis-planning on capacity and expected income.</p><p>### Actions Required</p><p>- Confirm and “own” the purchases target pace (**1.5/week**) and align weekly activity expectations accordingly.</p><p>- Fix reporting mismatch (apps vs split incl. NTUs) to ensure projections are evidence-based.</p><p>- Validate lead flow assumption and operational capacity to deliver conversation targets alongside renewals.</p><p>### Action Owner (Adviser / Admin / Case Manager / Other)</p><p>- Target ownership and fee execution on purchases: **Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>- Reporting integrity + forecasting: **Other (Bob)**</p><p>- Lead flow validation: **Other (Bob) + Adviser (Roland)**</p><p>### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving** — more realistic target ownership and grounded fee assumptions, but still constrained by lead supply and reporting integrity.</p><p>---</p><p>## Overall Performance Diagnosis (Based on available data)</p><p>### Adviser Strengths This Review</p><p>- Strong stated conversion and current run rate (“fantastic run rate”).</p><p>- Realistic calibration and willingness to be measured against clear activity targets (**1.5 new cases/week**, **6 purchases/month**).</p><p>- Positive engagement example: **Patrick Edington** completing fact find online, enabling faster affordability assessment within Platform.</p><p>### Main Risks</p><p>- Lead volume and consistency is the single largest threat (current **25**, average **15–20** vs desired **40**; model references **~480/year**).</p><p>- Reporting inconsistency (apps vs purchases/remortgages; likely NTUs) risks misleading planning and accountability.</p><p>- Admin continuity risk due to reliance on **Megan** and maternity leave concern.</p><p>- Month-by-month purchase consistency required to avoid quiet-period slippage (quieter periods referenced: **November–January**).</p><p>### Behaviour Patterns</p><p>- Positive: moving towards structured workflow-based follow-up and clearer weekly activity ownership.</p><p>- Risk: workflow adoption is fragile if tasks aren’t progressed (“click done”) and notes aren’t consistently added, creating ownership gaps with admin.</p><p>### Accountability Concerns</p><p>- No explicit client-level missed follow-ups or SLA breaches quantified in the provided content; however, **process risk of stalled hand-offs** is explicitly stated (task not progressed → admin not triggered).</p><p>### Where Management Support Is Required</p><p>- Stabilise/increase lead flow (dependency on wider team activity; **Alan** referenced).</p><p>- Ensure admin cover plan is in place (Megan continuity risk).</p><p>- Resolve reporting integrity (apps vs purchases/remortgages; NTUs).</p><p>- Issue quarterly targets pack (written/banked and case targets) to enable week-on-week performance tracking.</p><p>---</p><p>## Master Action List</p><p>| Action | Client / Area | Owner | Deadline | Risk if not done |</p><p>| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |</p><p>| Investigate and fix reporting inconsistency (**74 apps FY** vs **24 purchases / 15 remortgages**; likely NTUs included) and confirm correct baseline | Reporting / MI | Other (Bob) | Not stated | Targets and capacity plan built on incorrect data; misallocation of effort |</p><p>| Confirm and own 2026 target: **72 purchase cases/year** (≈ **1.5/week**) and align activity expectations to deliver it | New business plan | Adviser (Roland) | Not stated | Under-delivery due to inconsistent weekly activity and lack of commitment |</p><p>| Validate lead flow assumption (**~480/year**) and feasibility of **30–35 conversations/month** alongside renewals | Lead supply &amp; workload | Other (Bob) + Adviser (Roland) | Not stated | Income plan becomes unattainable if lead volume/contact rate is lower |</p><p>| Build quarterly forecast showing written vs banked distribution (using last year’s banking pattern referenced) | Income planning to **£178,680** | Other (Bob Duncan) | 2026-04-23 | Target drift; inability to spot shortfall early; higher slippage risk |</p><p>| Build purchase seasonality forecast (where purchases land across the year) | Purchase consistency to **72/year** | Other (Bob Duncan) | 2026-04-23 | Underperformance in quieter months without mitigation plan |</p><p>| Maintain activity pace: **1.5 new cases/week** and **6 purchase cases/month** | New business | Adviser (Roland Brownlee) | Ongoing (from 2026-04-08) | Missed annual purchase count (72) and income target (£178,680) |</p><p>| Implement Platform task-based workflow for lead follow-up and nurture hand-offs | Lead management / CRM discipline | Adviser (Roland) + Admin (Megan/Admin team) | From 2026-04-20 | Leads drift; inconsistent follow-up; increased NPWs; ownership gaps |</p><p>| Practice Platform navigation using demo case **John Smith** (add/remove journeys; workflow steps) | Training / system adoption | Adviser (Roland) + Admin (Megan) | 2026-04-10 | Low adoption; continued reliance on informal tracking; stalled hand-offs |</p><p>| Calculate and share quarterly targets for written/banked + case targets | Targets / performance framework | Other (Bob Duncan) | 2026-04-10 (or w/c 2026-04-13 if delayed) | Lack of clarity on weekly run-rate; reduced accountability |</p><p>| Confirm admin cover plan given Megan maternity leave concern | Resourcing | Other (Ops/Management) | 2026-05-06 | Follow-up gaps; reduced appointment rebooking; pipeline leakage |</p><p>---</p><p>## Email Recap (Auto-Generated From Review)</p><p>**Subject:** Weekly Performance Review and Agreed Actions</p><p>Hi Roland,</p><p>Below is a summary of this week’s review and agreed actions.</p><p>### Follow-Up From Last Week</p><p>- **Not available** — no prior-week action list was provided in the content, so completion vs outstanding cannot be evidenced.</p><p>### Performance Snapshot</p><p>- Annual target agreed: **£178,680**</p><p>- Activity plan: **72 purchases/year** (**1.5 new cases/week** = **6 purchases/month**), **48 renewals**, **48 insurance cases**, **96 admin fees**</p><p>- Written requirement discussed to bank target: **~£195k written** (avg **just over £16k/month**)</p><p>- Current written run rate referenced: last 3 months written **£42k** → annualised **~£168k**</p><p>- Banked performance (YTD) stated: **~£32,000** (Mortgage **£16,000**; Renewal **£1,200**; Insurance **£7,000**; additional line **~£7,500**)</p><p